// Title: ESXi System Information Discovery Via ESXCLI
// Sigma ID: e80273e1-9faf-40bc-bd85-dbaff104c4e9
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Cedric Maurugeon
// Date: 2023-09-04 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: discovery, execution, T1033, T1007, T1059.012
// Description: Detects execution of the "esxcli" command with the "system" flag in order to retrieve
//   information about the different component of the system. Such as accounts, modules, NTP, etc.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate administration activities
// References:
//   - https://www.crowdstrike.com/blog/hypervisor-jackpotting-ecrime-actors-increase-targeting-of-esxi-servers/
//   - https://developer.broadcom.com/xapis/esxcli-command-reference/7.0.0/namespace/esxcli_system.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_esxcli_system_discovery.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "/esxcli" and ProcessCommandLine contains "system") and (ProcessCommandLine contains " get" or ProcessCommandLine contains " list")
